{
  "subject": {
    "slug": "research-and-evidence-based-practice",
    "title": "Research and Evidence Based Practice",
    "order": 10
  },
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "research-design",
    "title": "Research Design",
    "order": 0,
    "pages": [
      {
        "title": "The Blueprint for Your Study",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "A research design is the architectural blueprint of a study \u2014 it determines exactly how you'll collect and analyze data to answer your research question. Choosing the wrong design doesn't just make a study weaker; it can make the question un-answerable no matter how good your data collection is."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Three Approaches to Data",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "The type of question you ask dictates the type of data you need:"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "bulletList",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Quantitative",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 numerical data, statistical procedures, testing hypotheses and causal relationships"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Qualitative",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 nonnumerical data (interviews, focus groups) exploring subjective experiences or complex phenomena in depth"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Mixed-Methods",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 combines both to give a more complete answer than either alone"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Descriptive Studies",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Descriptive studies",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " collect data without changing or manipulating the environment \u2014 you observe and describe, such as a survey or case study. They're excellent for establishing baseline understanding of a situation, but they can't establish cause and effect."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "True Experimental Design",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "callout",
                  "attrs": {
                    "kind": "key-principle"
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "A "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "true experimental",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " study introduces a treatment or procedure to test a cause-and-effect hypothesis. It requires four strict elements: "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "manipulation",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " of a variable, a "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "control group",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": ", "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "random assignment",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": ", and "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "random selection",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "In a clinical setting, hitting all four of these is often genuinely difficult \u2014 you frequently can't randomly assign patients to receive or not receive a treatment believed to be beneficial."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Quasi-Experimental Design",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "In clinical settings, assigning people to specific conditions is sometimes impossible or unethical. "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Quasi-experimental",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " research still evaluates cause-and-effect relationships, but without the strict randomization of a true experiment."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Example: comparing patient outcomes between two different, pre-existing hospital locations \u2014 you didn't randomly assign patients to each location, but you can still compare outcomes between the groups that already exist."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "knowledge_check",
            "key": "lq_page5_1",
            "stem": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "You use a phantom to compare VMAT delivery times when the gantry rotates clockwise vs. counterclockwise. What approach and design does this represent?"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "options": [
              "Qualitative descriptive",
              "Quantitative experimental"
            ],
            "answer": 1,
            "explanation": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "You're gathering numerical data (time) and actively manipulating a variable (rotation direction) in a controlled setting \u2014 that's quantitative and experimental, not just observational."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Matching Design to Real-World Constraints",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "The \"best\" design on paper isn't always the feasible one. A true experimental design with full randomization might be methodologically ideal but practically impossible to get approved or to execute with your available patient population, timeframe, or access. Choosing a design is always a negotiation between rigor and what you can actually pull off responsibly."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Evaluating a Source's Design",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "When you're reading someone else's published research, identifying their design tells you a lot about how much weight to put on their conclusions. A descriptive study showing an association is not the same evidence as a true experimental study showing causation \u2014 even if both papers report a \"statistically significant\" result."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Wrap-Up",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "You've covered quantitative, qualitative, and mixed-methods approaches, plus descriptive, true experimental, and quasi-experimental designs, and how feasibility shapes design choice in practice."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Next: once you've picked a design, you need to precisely define and measure the variables that design depends on."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
